course: dictionary
lesson: 1551
title: Champ: a winner, a friend, a chewer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Champ is short for champion, so it is an informal word for a winner. When a parent says, «Well done, champ, you won the race!», the word tells you the praise is friendly. The clue is the praise around it: well done and you won.
explain: Champ can also be a verb. The clues come from the animal: «The horse champed on its oats» means the horse chewed noisily. Champing at the bit is a phrase from horses that wait, stamping and chewing, before a race. It means being so eager to start that you can hardly wait.
explain: You may see French sentences with champ in them, such as «les chevaux sont dans le champ» (the horses are in the field) and «les Champs-Élysées» (a famous avenue in Paris). Those are French words, so do not mix them up with the English word here. Always check the language and the clues.
fact: Champ is a short, friendly form of champion, a winner.
fact: «Good job, champ!» is a friendly way to praise someone, often a child, for doing well.
fact: As a verb, champ means to bite or chew noisily, as horses do.
fact: Champing at the bit means being very impatient and eager to start.
fact: The plural of champ is champs, as in «The two champs shook hands.»
exercise: «Well done, champ, you won the race!» What does champ mean here? | A field where horses run | *A winner, said in a friendly way | A loud noise made by a crowd | A horse that is hungry
exercise: Horses stood at the gate, champing at the bit to be let out. What does champing at the bit mean? | Running away from the farm | *Very impatient and eager to start | Lying down to sleep | Chewing hay very quietly
exercise: «The horse champed on its oats.» What does champed mean? | Jumped over a fence | *Chewed noisily | Won a prize | Slept under a tree
test: Which word is the plural of champ? | champies | champes | *champs | champ's
test: «Pip was champing at the bit before the bell rang.» What does this tell you? | Pip was lost in the playground | Pip was chewing a sandwich | *Pip was very eager to start | Pip was tired and sleepy
exam: Mum says, «You are a champ for tidying up!» What does Mum mean? | She wants you to chew your dinner more slowly | She is telling you to act like a racehorse | *She is praising you in a friendly way | She is cross with you for being messy
